				"name": "Markus Unterwaditzer",
				"url": "https://gts.woodland.cafe/@untitaker",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/113/283/332/981/962/640/original/68be680a5d8ef6f5.png"
			},
			"date": "2026-03-18T16:31:42.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> at work it is, ports regularly conflict across services and their respective dependencies. particularly if you want to develop two services at once and each of them should have a dedicated redis or postgres instance for the sake of isolation, you now have to run one of the redises on a non-standard port which adds more config boilerplate. having one host per service would solve this. not convinced this portless thing is a good solution. HTTPS out of the box is a good aspect of it though, and the permission stuff is a non-issue IMO (again, for work... if a third-party OSS project imposed this onto me I would lose it)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
